<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Before you start</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    An AI policy is a short document that tells your team which AI tools they may use, what they may put into them and who is responsible when something goes wrong. It does not need legal language. It needs to be specific, easy to find and owned by a named person.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    This guide walks through seven steps, gives a policy outline you can adapt and ends with a checklist. External rules are quoted from the regulator pages listed in Sources. This is general information, not legal advice. Laws and guidance change, so check each point against the current regulator page and with qualified counsel before you act.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    It is different from our <a href="/resources/ai-governance-data-privacy-sme-whitepaper/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">SME governance checklist</a>, which covers data classification and vendor questions. This guide is about writing the policy document itself.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 1. Name an owner and a scope</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Pick one person who can approve tools and stop a use case. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework, released on 26 January 2023 for voluntary use, is organised around four functions (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage), and our ethical AI guide makes the same point: name an owner for each AI system, with authority to stop it.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    State who the policy covers: employees, contractors and anyone using AI on the company’s behalf.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 2. List the AI you actually use</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    You cannot govern what you have not listed. Make a simple table with the tool, the team using it, the data it receives and where that data is processed. Include features inside tools you already pay for, not only standalone chatbots.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The EU’s Article 50 duties apply, as summarised by Cooley, to AI that interacts with people, that generates synthetic content or that is used for emotion recognition. The list tells you which of those situations your company is in.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 3. Set data rules</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Decide which data may go into which tool. Use three plain categories and name examples for your business.
  </p>
  <div class="mb-5 overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-left"><thead><tr><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Category</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Examples to name</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Rule to state</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Public</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Published web copy, public price lists</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">May be used in approved tools</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Internal</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Meeting notes, internal plans</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Approved tools only</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Restricted</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Customer personal data, credentials, contracts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Only in tools approved for that category, after the checks in Step 4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Our own suggestion, not a legal requirement: make the approved route easy, because staff share data with unapproved tools mostly out of convenience.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 4. Check where data goes and who is accountable</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Regulators ask the same questions of any outside processor. Canada’s regulator says an organisation is responsible for personal information transferred to a third party for processing and must provide comparable protection through contractual or other means. Australia’s APP 8 requires reasonable steps to ensure an overseas recipient does not breach the APPs. The ICO says a restricted transfer from the UK must be covered by adequacy regulations, appropriate safeguards or an exception.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Put the supplier questions into the policy as a standing approval step: countries of storage and processing, sub-processors, transfer mechanism, access by foreign authorities, breach notice time, deletion at exit. If a tool can act on your systems, add the permission checks in our <a href="/resources/mcp-agent-security-checklist-business-whitepaper/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">agent security checklist</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 5. Add review and disclosure rules</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li><strong>Human review:</strong> state which outputs must be checked by a person before they reach a customer. The UNESCO Recommendation says AI systems should not displace ultimate human responsibility and accountability.</li>
    <li><strong>Disclosure:</strong> say when staff must tell people they are dealing with AI. For EU-facing work, Article 50 transparency duties have applied since 2 August 2026, with a grace period to 2 December 2026 for marking AI-generated content in systems already on the market.</li>
    <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> NIST’s generative AI profile (July 2024) describes confabulation as confidently stated but erroneous or false content, so require checking of facts, figures and quotes.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 6. Define incident handling</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Say what counts as an incident, who to tell and how fast. Examples: sensitive data pasted into an unapproved tool, an AI output that reached a customer with a material error, or a supplier reporting a breach.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Australia’s Notifiable Data Breaches scheme requires covered organisations to notify affected individuals and the OAIC when a breach involving personal information is likely to result in serious harm. If you operate there, your incident steps must be fast enough to support that.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 7. Train, publish and review</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Walk the team through the policy with real examples from their work. Publish it where people already look. Record who has read it.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Our own suggestion: review every six months and whenever a tool, supplier or law changes. Note the dates you are watching. For the EU AI Act, the Digital Omnibus on AI, in force since 27 July 2026, moved Annex III high-risk obligations to 2 December 2027 and Annex I to 2 August 2028.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Policy outline you can adapt</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Our own suggestion, not a legal requirement. Use these headings and keep each to a few lines.
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li><strong>1. Purpose and scope:</strong> why the policy exists and who it covers.</li>
    <li><strong>2. Owner:</strong> the named person and how to reach them.</li>
    <li><strong>3. Approved tools:</strong> a table, with the data category each may receive.</li>
    <li><strong>4. Data rules:</strong> public, internal and restricted, with examples.</li>
    <li><strong>5. Approval of new tools:</strong> the supplier questions from Step 4.</li>
    <li><strong>6. Review and disclosure:</strong> what needs human checking and what needs a customer notice.</li>
    <li><strong>7. Incidents:</strong> what to report, to whom and by when.</li>
    <li><strong>8. Training and review date:</strong> when staff are briefed and when the policy is next reviewed.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Checklist before you publish</h2>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>An owner is named and has authority to stop a use.</li>
    <li>Every AI tool in use is listed with where its data is processed.</li>
    <li>Restricted data categories are defined with examples.</li>
    <li>Each approved tool has a written answer on storage location, sub-processors and access.</li>
    <li>Customer-facing AI discloses that it is AI where required.</li>
    <li>Outputs that reach customers have a named reviewer.</li>
    <li>The incident route and timing are written down.</li>
    <li>A review date is in the calendar.</li>
    <li>A qualified lawyer has looked at the parts that depend on your market.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Need help assessing a specific tool or building one with these controls? You can <a href="/schedule-a-call/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">schedule a call</a> or read about our <a href="/solutions/custom-agent/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">custom AI agent work</a>.
  </p>
</div>

